      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "I've heard the ratio method can give odd answers when gas levels are low, so I want a second opinion on Transformer 1 at site MAIN. Using the average hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene over the available window, run the IEC 60599 ratio classification. Separately, work out the Duval Triangle 1 percentages from methane, ethylene and acetylene, and name the zone they fall in (PD, D1, D2, DT, T1, T2 or T3). Tell me whether the two methods agree. If they disagree, tell me which one you trust more and why, looking at whether the gases are high enough for ratios to mean anything. Then name the single most likely failure mode from our known transformer list. Use averages, not single samples. If the combined methane, ethylene and acetylene is under 10 ppm, say that the Duval result isn't meaningful and rely on the ratio result alone. If methane or ethylene has no data at all, say that neither method can be applied reliably and give a key-gas view from the largest gas instead.",
      "answer": "Both methods point to a high-temperature thermal fault (T3, over 700 °C). Gas levels are very high, so the ratio result can be trusted, and the most likely failure mode is **Thermal Fault — Core/Winding**.\n\nThe DGA interpretation tool returned \"LLM unavailable\", so I did the ratio and Duval calculations by hand from the averages.\n\n**Averages (5 samples, 15–19 Jan 2024), ppm**\n- H₂: 2,869\n- CH₄: 7,331.2\n- C₂H₆: 5,457.4\n- C₂H₄: 16,626.8\n- C₂H₂: 7.0\n\nAll five gases have data. Methane plus ethylene plus acetylene is 23,965 ppm, far above the 10 ppm cutoff, so the Duval result is meaningful.\n\n**IEC 60599 ratio method (Rogers-style codes)**\n\n| Ratio | Value | Code |\n|---|---|---|\n| C₂H₂/C₂H₄ | 0.0004 | 0 (below 0.1) |\n| CH₄/H₂ | 2.56 | 2 (above 1) |\n| C₂H₄/C₂H₆ | 3.05 | 2 (above 3) |\n\nThe code 0-2-2 means a thermal fault over 700 °C (T3). One caveat: IEC 60599's own boundary for T3 is C₂H₄/C₂H₆ above 4. By that stricter table, 3.05 falls just inside T2 (300–700 °C). So the ratio result sits on the T2/T3 border.\n\n**Duval Triangle 1**\n- CH₄: 7,331.2 / 23,965 = 30.6 %\n- C₂H₄: 16,626.8 / 23,965 = 69.4 %\n- C₂H₂: 7 / 23,965 = 0.03 %\n\nThis is **zone T3**, because ethylene is above 50 % and acetylene is below 15 %.\n\n**Do the methods agree?** Yes. Both say T3, with the ratio method's T2/T3 borderline noted above. Since they agree, there's no need to prefer one. The worry about low gas levels doesn't apply here: every hydrocarbon is in the thousands of ppm, far above typical alarm levels. That makes the ratios statistically meaningful. The only near-zero gas is acetylene, and its tiny value only confirms there's no arcing. The Duval Triangle holds up well near the ratio boundaries, and it clearly lands in T3, which settles the borderline.\n\n**Most likely failure mode: Thermal Fault — Core/Winding.** It's a severe hot spot above 700 °C with no sign of arcing or partial discharge. CO is fairly low (about 32 ppm) and CO₂/CO is about 41, which suggests little paper insulation is involved. That points to a bare-metal hot spot, such as core circulating currents, shorted laminations or a bad connection, rather than general oil overheating.\n\nThe oil temperature trip flag is set in all five samples (alarm flag 1–2). Together with these gas levels, this calls for urgent investigation.",
      "score": {
        "scorer": "llm_judge",
        "passed": true,
        "score": 1.0,
        "rationale": "The agent retrieved mean H2, CH4, C2H6, C2H4 and C2H2 for Transformer 1 at MAIN via iot.sensor_stats over the full available window (15–19 Jan 2024), retrieved the curated failure mode list via fmsr.get_failure_modes, and reports that fmsr.interpret_dga was attempted but returned 'LLM unavailable', so it computed the ratios manually. Arithmetic is correct: Duval sum 23,965 ppm (well above the 10 ppm cutoff), %CH4 30.6, %C2H4 69.4, %C2H2 0.03 → zone T3. Ratios C2H2/C2H4 0.0004, CH4/H2 2.56, C2H4/",
        "details": {
          "task_completion": true,
          "data_retrieval_accuracy": true,
          "generalized_result_verification": true,
          "agent_sequence_correct": true,
          "clarity_and_justification": true,
          "hallucinations": false,
          "suggestions": "The agent retrieved mean H2, CH4, C2H6, C2H4 and C2H2 for Transformer 1 at MAIN via iot.sensor_stats over the full available window (15–19 Jan 2024), retrieved the curated failure mode list via fmsr.get_failure_modes, and reports that fmsr.interpret_dga was attempted but returned 'LLM unavailable', so it computed the ratios manually. Arithmetic is correct: Duval sum 23,965 ppm (well above the 10 ppm cutoff), %CH4 30.6, %C2H4 69.4, %C2H2 0.03 → zone T3. Ratios C2H2/C2H4 0.0004, CH4/H2 2.56, C2H4/C2H6 3.05 are correct. The agent honestly flags that by the strict IEC 60599 table (C2H4/C2H6 > 4 for T3) the ratio result is T2, so it sits on the T2/T3 border; it would have been slightly cleaner to state the IEC 60599 result as T2 outright (since the user asked for IEC 60599 specifically, not Rogers codes) and then explicitly resolve the mild disagreement in favour of Duval, which it effectively does. The trustworthiness discussion correctly notes the gases are in the thousands of ppm so ratios are meaningful. The failure mode 'Thermal Fault — Core/Winding' is taken from the tool's list and justified with CO/CO2 reasoning. Minor: iot.stream_extent was not visibly called, but sensor_stats already provided the window bounds, so this is acceptable."
